# The bit tuples of the JVM's value types, the Java FFI's own representation, faithful to the JVM and apart from the
# standard library's numbers, which it leaves unchanged: a value type of width w is the Scott tuple of its w
# bits, high bit first, each a Scott boolean, the JVM's boolean itself. The tuples are by width alone: byte is
# bits_8; short and char are bits_16; int and float are bits_32; long and double are bits_64, the bits of a float or a
# double being the IEEE 754 bits Float.floatToRawIntBits and Double.doubleToRawLongBits give. Two types of one width
# have one criterion, since a criterion is a term and two α-equivalent terms are one (`CONTRIBUTING.md`, "Structure only: no names, and every function a pure function of the term":
# "不允许使用名字，所有东西都必须按结构的纯函数"); a construct's descriptor says how its bits are read, as a JVM instruction's
# opcode does.
#
# Each width has its constructor, which makes a tuple of w bits, and its criterion, the tuples of w booleans, the tuple
# of w copies of the booleans' criterion; a filter x ∥ bits_32_type in a term asserts that x is a tuple of 32 bits.
# The families are declared in corpora/compiler/java_ffi.lambdalattice.
